Bugfix: Run the PROPFIND share-read check before Sabre answers

FilesPlugin registered checkPropFind() for method:PROPFIND without a priority,
so it sat at Sabre's default of 100 - the same priority as Sabre's own
httpPropFind handler. Sabre registers that handler while the server is
constructed, i.e. before this plugin is initialized, so at an equal priority it
wins on insertion order, answers the request and stops propagation. The check
never ran.

What the check does is reject a PROPFIND on a share whose permissions do not
include read: that is what keeps an upload-only ("file drop") share from being
listed by the person uploading into it. With the handler unreachable, the
contents of such a share were listable over WebDAV.

It is now registered at priority 99, as upstream does. A regression test pins
the priority, because nothing else in the plugin's tests would notice its loss.

Taken over from upstream owncloud/core.

https://github.com/owncloud/core/pull/41676
